# Untitled

Interfacial $\pmb{\pi}$ -Electron Cloud Extension and Charge Transfer Between Preferable Single-Crystalline Conjugated MOFs and Graphene for Ultrafast Pulse Generation
2D conjugated metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted significant attention in various fields due to their outstanding characteristics. However, due to the strong interlayer $\pmb{\pi}\mathrm{-}\pmb{\pi}$ stacking interactions, the preparation of high-quality and atomic-scale single-crystalline conjugated MOF structures continues to pose a significant challenge. The investigation of its nonlinear optical (NLO) property and application for ultrafast photonics is still rare. Herein, the ultrathin $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ $(\mathsf{H H T P}=2,3,6,7,\mathsf{70},\mathsf{71},$ - hexahydroxytriphenylene) nanosheets (CuHHTPNs and NiHHTPNs) with single-crystalline characteristic are prepared by surfactant-assisted solution synthesis strategy. Moreover, the $\pmb{\pi}\mathrm{-}\pmb{\pi}$ stacked CuHHTPNs(NiHHTPNs)/ graphene van der Waals heterostructures (CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS) are achieved by ultrasound-assisted method. According to characterization analyses and theoretical simulations, this preferable stacking ultrathin van der Waals heterostructures exhibits superior $\pmb{\pi}$ -conjugated electron cloud extension, charge transfer, and NLO properties. Noticeably, the third-order NLO polarizability of CuNsG-VHS keeps in a relatively high level compared with the reported 2D saturable absorber materials in the near-infrared wavelength range. Based on these outstanding properties, CuNsG-VHS can serve as an excellent saturable absorber to achieve fundamental mode-locking with femtosecond pulse duration, and high-order harmonic mode-locking with GHz repetition frequency. These demonstrations provide a valuable strategy for the development of promising conjugated MOFs for ultrafast photonics and advanced optoelectronic devices.
## 1. Introduction
Ultrafast fiber lasers are widely used in ultra-precision manufacturing,[1] medical diagnostics, laser processing,[2] optical communication,[3] spectroscopy,[4] nonlinear optical imaging,[5] and other related areas,[6] owing to their small size, high peak power, short pulse width, and good compactness. For generating an ultrafast pulse laser, passive mode-locking based on a saturable absorber (SA) as a key component is a crucial technique. Semiconductor saturable absorber mirrors (SESAM) is a long-researched SA that is used commercially,[7] which has limitations including slow saturation recovery time, narrow operating bandwidth, and complex preparation process. Recently, various types of 2D nanomaterials including graphene, transition-metal disulfide compounds (TMDs), topological insulators (TIs), transparent metal oxides, black phosphorus (BP), and MXenes have been witnessed their extensively potential for ultrafast photonics owing to broadband optical absorption, ultrafast optical response and strong optical nonlinearity.[8–10] However, despite the outstanding saturable absorption properties of these 2D nanomaterials and their remarkable achievements in the field of ultrafast photonics, it is difficult to achieve large nonlinear absorption coefficient, strong chemical stability, and high-quality single-crystal structure all in one material to meet the demand for higher performance of optoelectronic devices which brought about by the rapid development of modern optoelectronic systems. Therefore, there is an urgent need to explore novel 2D materials as nonlinear media for ultrafast pulsed laser generation.
In recent years, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as new classes of 2D materials, have found important applications in catalysis,[11] gas storage,[12] drug delivery,[13] and energy storage systems[14] due to their large specific surface area, stable and controllable structure, and well-designed primary ligands. Nevertheless, the low conductivity of these conventional MOFs restricts their development for optoelectronic applications.
Moreover, the previously reported 2D MOFs prepared via the solvothermal method generally display the bulk powder morphology. The polycrystalline feature of these conventional powder materials is not conducive to the transmission of charge carriers. In contrast, nanosheets can increase the crystal quality, shorten the electron transport path by quantum confinement effect, and accelerate the separation and transport of photogenerated electrons.[20] Accordingly, different kinds of methods including template-assisted synthesis,[21] bottom-up interface-assisted route,[22,23] and top-down physical/chemical exfoliation of 2D bulk layered crystal,[24,25] have been applied to synthesize nonconjugated 2D ultrathin MOFs nanosheets. However, due to the strong interlayer $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions of bulk 2D conjugated MOFs, the exfoliation of these layered materials and preparation of high-quality and atomic-scale conjugated MOFs nanosheets via controllable solution processing are still difficult. Therefore, the fabrication of ultrathin 2D conjugated MOFs nanosheets with single-crystalline features, high stability, and large-size is still an attractive target and a great challenge.
In the meanwhile, to enhance the nonlinear optical (NLO) properties of the material, constructing heterostructure is regarded as an effective way. This approach is generally achieved via the interactions of two substances by van der Waals forces and provides considerable benefits in improving their NLO properties. Compared with the individual materials, the heterostructures retained the distinctive features of each material and displayed preferable NLO properties.[26–28] Given that 2D conjugated MOFs serve as analogues to graphene and possess the ability to form heterostructure through $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction, the approach of developing $\pi{\cdot}\pi$ conjugated heterostructures using $\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ in conjunction with graphene appears to be a viable strategy. Since the delocalization of $\pi$ -electron clouds is beneficial for the enhancement of third-order NLO polarizability, the construction of such $\pi$ -conjugated heterostructures may increase the $\pi$ -electron delocalization and electron cloud density, leading to superior NLO performance.[29]
Herein, the ultrathin 2D d- $\pi$ conjugated $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ $\mathrm{\langleHHTP}=2,3,6,7,10,11$ -hexahydroxytriphenylene) MOFs nanosheets (denoted as CuHHTPNs and NiHHTPNs) were prepared by surfactant-assisted solution synthesis strategy. The nanosheets displayed single-crystalline characteristics with a thickness of $3{-}4~\mathrm{nm}$ . Subsequently, ultrasound-assisted method was employed to obtain $\pi{\cdot}\pi$ stacked CuHHTPNs/graphene and NiHHTPNs/graphene van der Waals heterostructures (CuNsGVHS and NiNsG-VHS). According to characterization analyses and theoretical simulations, a rapid charge transfer and a $\pi$ - conjugated electron clouds extension were achieved at the interface of heterostructures. Moreover, the nonlinear transmission curve and openaperture Z-scan measurement showed that the modulation depth, nonlinear absorption coefficient, and thirdorder nonlinear polarizability were greatly improved after the formation of $\pi{\cdot}\pi$ stacking interactions. The delocalization effect and $\pi$ -conjugated electron clouds extension caused the improvement of NLO properties for CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS. Using the ring-cavity Er-doped fiber laser, CuNsG-VHS realized fundamental mode-locking (FML) with a pulse duration of 438 fs, 26-order harmonic mode-locking (HML), and repetition frequency as high as 1.26 GHz. NiNsG-VHS achieved FML with a pulse duration of 449 fs, 18-order HML, and repetition frequency of $0.87\:\mathrm{GHz}$ In addition, the ultrathin MOFs nanosheets stacked graphene exhibited preferable performance than bulk MOFs stacked graphene. The ultrathin single-crystal property of CuHHTPNs and NiHHTPNs is beneficial for $\pi{\cdot}\pi$ stacking and forming heterostructures, which is more conducive to interlayer charge transfer. These results indicate that CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS can be served as superior nonlinear optical materials for the development of future optoelectronic devices.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Material Preparation and Characterization
The ultrathin 2D conjugated MOFs nanosheets were prepared via a surfactant-assisted synthesis strategy. The schematic diagram is illustrated in Figure 1a. In a typical synthesis, the uniform ultrathin CuHHTPNs and NiHHTPNs were obtained using sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) as the anionic surfactant. Supporting Information 1.1, 1.2 and Figure S1 (Supporting Information) depict the details of the preparation process. As shown in Figure 1a and Figure S3 (Supporting Information), the obtained CuHHTPNs and NiHHTPNs nanosheets colloidal suspension displayed the Tyndall effect. In Figure 1b and Figure S3a (Supporting Information), the atomic force microscopy (AFM) images demonstrated that they displayed the smooth surface and nanosheet morphology. The thickness of CuHHTPNs and NiHHTPNs was measured to be $3{\-}4~\mathrm{nm}$ , and the grain size reached ${\approx}1~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ . Figure S4 (Supporting Information) shows the EDX mapping images of the samples. A uniform distribution of C, Cu, and O was observed for CuHHTPNs and a uniform distribution of C, Ni, and O was seen in the NiHHTPNs. The HRTEM and selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) are revealed in Figure 1c and Figure S2 (Supporting Information) to gain insight into the crystal structure of CuHHTPNs. The bright diffraction spots with hexagonal symmetry were observed in the SAED pattern, demonstrating that the isolated CuHHTPNs nanosheet displayed the single-crystalline characteristic.[30,31] The calculated unit cell parameter (a) from first-order reflection in the SAED pattern was $2.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which was in agreement with the lattice parameter of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ hexagonal structure.[32,33] Moreover, in Figure S2e (Supporting Information), the highly ordered hexagonal $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ honeycomb structure was seen in the HRTEM image. The pore size of CuHHTPNs was $2.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which was completely consistent with the structural model and previous literatures.[33,34] The $\mathrm{x}.$ ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) patterns of CuHHTPNs and NiHHTPNs are shown in Figures S5 and S6 (Supporting Information). CuHHTPNs and NiHHTPNs showed consistent characteristic peaks with reported $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ materials.[35–37] Figures S7 and S8 (Supporting Information) exhibit the FT-IR spectra of HHTP, CuHHTPNs, and NiHHTPNs. The peaks at below $1000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to metal-O stretching modes.[37–39] Compared with HHTP ligand, the peaks observed at $692\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for CuHHTPNs and $670\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for NiHHTPNs were belonged to the newly formed $\mathrm{Cu-O}$ and Ni─O bonds, respectively.[39] All the aforementioned results proved the formation of MOF structure.
During the reaction process, the hydrophilic tails with negative charges in SDS molecules exhibited a strong tendency to bind to the MOF surfaces via electrostatic interactions.[40] Consequently, these SDS molecules function as a structural directing agent, effectively promoting anisotropic growth along the 2D plane and mitigating layer stacking, which results in the generation of ultrathin MOFs nanosheets after sonication. Moreover, the SDS played a crucial role in stabilizing the prepared nanosheets in solution and preventing further agglomeration.[23,41] For comparison, conventional bulk $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ powders (denoted as CuHHTPB and NiHHTPB) were fabricated in the absence of the surfactant (Supporting Information 1.3 and 1.4 for experimental details, Figures S7–S10 (Supporting Information) for detailed characterizations).
The $\pi{-}\pi$ stacked CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS were prepared by ultrasonic-assisted self-assembly route. Figure 1d reveals the schematic diagram and Supporting Information 1.5 shows the details of preparation process. Figure 1e,f, and Figure S11 (Supporting Information) show the AFM image, SEM image, metallurgical microscope image, and TEM image of CuNsG-VHS. It can be clearly observed that isolated CuHHTPNs nanosheets were uniformly distributed on the surface of graphene without the appearance of clusters and agglomeration. In Figure 1e and Figure S11a (Supporting Information), the height of the CuHHTPNs nanosheets on graphene matched that of individual nanosheets, demonstrating the formation of ultrathin $\pi{-}\pi$ stacked van der Waals heterostructure. Figure S11d (Supporting Information) reveals the HRTEM images of CuNsG-VHS. The lattice spacing of $2.0\mathrm{nm}$ was attributed to the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ (100) crystal plane, and the lattice spacing of $0.21\ \mathrm{nm}$ represented the graphene (100) crystal plane. The XRD patterns of CuHHTPNs, graphene, and CuNsG-VHS powders are shown in Figure S12a (Supporting Information). The diffraction peak of graphene nanosheet stacking was appeared at $26.33^{\circ}$ . The $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking diffraction peak of CuNsG-VHS was observed at $26.54^{\circ}$ . The slight shift of the peak was attributed to the generation of $\pi{\cdot}\pi$ stacked heterostructures between CuHHTPNs and graphene, which altered the electronic structure and influenced the stacking conditions.[27] Figure S12b (Supporting Information) shows the Raman spectra of the samples. The p-type doping state was achieved for graphene due to the blueshift of G and 2D peaks, demonstrating electrons transferred from graphene to CuHHTPNs.[42] The interface charge transfer caused via $\pi$ - $\pi$ interaction further proved the generation of CuNsG-VHS heterostructure. UV–Vis-NIR spectrophotometer was used to measure the linear absorption spectra. In Figure S12c and S14c (Supporting Information), all the samples showed broadband linear absorption in the range $400{-}2000\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Compared with the individual materials, CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS displayed a significant enhancement in absorption. Accordingly, the light-capturing capacity and electron transfer were promoted due to $\pi$ -conjugated electron clouds extension within the heterostructures, thus enhancing the linear absorption. Moreover, in Figure S17 (Supporting Information), CuNsG-VHS and NiNsGVHS showed stronger absorption intensity than CuBG-VHS and NiBG-VHS, respectively. These results indicated that the preferable stacking for ultrathin nanosheets and graphene nanosheets may lead to larger $\pi$ -conjugated electron cloud, more charge transfer and stronger absorption. More detailed characterizations are displayed in Figures S11–S17 (Supporting Information).
The femtosecond transient absorption (TA) was carried out to investigate the carrier dynamics. As shown in Figure S18 (Supporting Information), CuHHTPNs had a long duration of bleaching signal between $1350{-}1450\ \mathrm{nm}$ . A faster recovery of the bleaching signal was achieved for CuNsG-VHS, indicating a higher carrier recombination speed. The dynamic curves of the samples at $1390\mathrm{nm}$ are shown in Figure 1g–i. The TA curves can be fitted via a double exponential function (Supporting Information 1.7) and the TA kinetic parameters at $1390\mathrm{nm}$ are given in Table S1 (Supporting Information). For CuHHTPNs, two decay time constants of $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ corresponding to fast and slow relaxation time were 808 fs $(52.94\%)$ and 20.79 ps $(47.06\%)$ , respectively. The $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ were greatly shortened after the formation of $\scriptstyle{\pi-\pi}$ stacked CuNsG-VHS heterostructure $(\tau_{1}=334$ fs $(69.79\%)$ and $\tau_{2}=3.35$ ps $(30.21\%)$ . The acceleration of fast and slow carrier recovery times was due to the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction and interface charge transfer. Moreover, the charge transfer rate $k_{E T}$ was $3.08\times10^{11}~\mathrm{{s^{-1}}}$ in CuNsG-VHS, further demonstrating the efficient charge transfer.
## 2.2. Theoretical Simulations and Analysis
Density functional theory (DFT) calculation was employed to conduct in-depth study of the properties of CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS. Figure 2a,b show the crystal structure of CuNsGVHS from side and top views, respectively. Figure 2c presents the band structure and density of states (DOS) of the CuNsGVHS, retaining the key characteristics of graphene. The $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction at the CuNsG-VHS interface causes the Dirac point to shift $0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ above the Fermi level (Figure S19, Supporting Information), indicating the electrons transfer from graphene to $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ .[43] Moreover, the charge transfer kinetics of CuNsG-VHS were studied via calculating the differential charges and Bader charges. As shown in Figures 2d,e, the green represented charge depletion areas, while the orange indicated charge accumulation regions. An obvious charge transfer from graphene to $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ was observed. The properties of NiNsG-VHS shown in Figure $2\mathrm{f-j}$ were similar to those described for CuNsG-VHS. The calculated Bader charge illustrated that the electron transfer from graphene to $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ was ${\approx}1.01$ e, which was larger than that from graphene to $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ $\mathrm{(\approx0.74~e)}$ . The Electron Localization Function (ELF) images of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ are given in Figure S20 (Supporting Information). The larger red area around the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ framework represented the higher electron density, which may be more conducive to charge transfer at the interface.
The interface movement mechanism between $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ and graphene is illustrated in Figure S21 (Supporting Information). Based on band theory, electron tends to migrate from materials with lower work functions to those with higher work functions. The calculated work functions of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ and graphene were 5.09 and $4.25\mathrm{eV},$ , respectively. When $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ and graphene come into contact, electron transfer occurs until their Fermi levels align. The redistribution of charge between $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ and graphene creates a potential difference, establishing an internal electric field directed from the graphene to the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ layer. The internal electric field can facilitate the efficient separation of photo-generated electronhole pairs, significantly enhancing the carrier mobility.[44,45] This observation corresponds with the work function curves presented in Figure S22 (Supporting Information). In the $\pi$ conjugated system, the extended $\pi$ orbitals and high electron mobility improve the migration and separation of carriers at the interface. It not only increases the photoelectric conversion efficiency but also enhances light absorption and NLO properties. Additionally, the similar results were obtained in Figures S22 and S23 (Supporting Information) for NiNsG-VHS heterostructure.
In Figures S24 and S25 (Supporting Information), two types of interfaces including ultrathin CuHHTPNs/graphene and bulk CuHHTPB/graphene are built. In Type-I, CuHHTPNs were arranged in a planar manner with graphene, corresponding to CuNsG-VHS. In Type-II, CuHHTPB was tilted at a specific angle to the graphene monolayer, corresponding to the interaction of CuBG-VHS. CuNsG-VHS exhibited a lower binding energy of $-0.94\ \mathrm{eV}$ than CuBG-VHS $(-0.12\ \mathrm{eV})$ , indicating the better bonding of CuNsG-VHS. Moreover, the charge transfer between CuHHTPNs and graphene was significantly more effective. The preferable stacking for ultrathin MOF nanosheets and graphene provided larger $\pi$ -conjugated electron cloud, more charge transfer, and stronger absorption. The superior interactions at the MOF/graphene interface were achieved for CuNsG-VHS, which may lead to better NLO properties and pulse output performance.
## 2.3. Nonlinear Optical Properties
Employing the double-balanced detector, the nonlinear transmittance curves of the SAs are exhibited in Figures 3a–e and S26 (Supporting Information). The diagram of double-balanced detector is given in Figure 3f. After fitting the nonlinear curve and calculation, the modulation depth of CuNsG-VHS was ${\approx}4.4$ times higher than CuHHTPNs and 3.3 times higher than graphene. The modulation depth of NiNsG-VHS was ${\approx}4.8$ times higher than NiHHTPNs and 2.9 times higher than graphene. The saturation power density was also enhanced after the formation of heterostructure. The result explicitly indicated that the NLO properties were significantly improved for $\pi{-}\pi$ stacked heterostructures. In addition, CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS revealed higher modulation depths than CuBG-VHS and NiBGVHS, respectively. The open-aperture Z-scan measurements were carried out to further confirm that $\pi{-}\pi$ stacked heterostructures could improve the third-order NLO properties of the materials. Figure 4 displays the Z-scan curves of various SAs at ${800}\mathrm{nm}$ . The nonlinear absorption coefficient $\beta$ of CuHHTPNs, graphene, and CuNsG-VHS were $-1.9\times10^{4}$ , $-2.3\times10^{4}$ and $-1.1$ $\times10^{5}\mathrm{~cm~GW^{-1}}$ respectively. The third-order nonlinear polarizability $\operatorname{Im}\chi^{(3)}$ of CuHHTPNs, graphene and CuNsG-VHS were −8.1, $-9.8$ , and −46.6 $(\times10^{-8})$ esu respectively. The $\beta$ and $\operatorname{Im}\chi^{(3)}$ of CuNsG-VHS were much higher than those of CuHHTPNs and graphene. NiNsG-VHS revealed the same trend of change. These results further demonstrated that $\pi{-}\pi$ stacked heterostructures greatly enhanced the NLO properties. Moreover, the $\beta$ values of CuBG-VHS and NiBG-VHS were $-4.2\times10^{4}$ and - $-3.8\times$ $10^{4}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}\thinspace\mathrm{GW}^{-1}$ , respectively. The $\operatorname{Im}\chi^{(3)}$ of CuBG-VHS and NiBGVHS were $-17.8$ and −16.1 $(\times10^{-8})$ esu, respectively. The ultrathin MOF nanosheets stacked graphene heterostructures exhibited superior third-order NLO properties compared with conventional bulk MOF stacked graphene, which was consistent with the results of nonlinear transmittance curves and theoretical simulations. More discussions were given in Supporting Information.
Moreover, the NLO parameters of different 2D materials are listed in Table 1. The $\beta$ and $\operatorname{Im}\chi^{(3)}$ values of CuNs-VHS at 800 nm were relatively high among the reported 2D SAs. Particularly, $\operatorname{Im}\chi^{(3)}$ of CuNs-VHS was three orders of magnitude greater than $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ ,[46,47] two orders of magnitude greater than $\mathrm{\WS}_{2}$ ,[48] one order of magnitude greater than ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{Se}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}_{1.6}\mathrm{Sb}_{0.4}\mathrm{Te}_{3}$ ,[49,50] 7 times greater than germanene,[51] and so on.[52] In the meantime, the figure of merit (FOM) was calculated by the following equation: $\mathrm{FOM}=|\mathrm{I}m\chi^{(3)}/\alpha_{0}|$ . The FOM of CuNs-VHS at $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ was also far gr|eater than the reported 2D SAs materials. Due to the fact that the length of $\pi$ -conjugation has a significant effect on NLO properties of the materials.[53] According to the equation $\chi^{(3)}\propto L^{10}/N^{3}$ , the increase of conjugate length L in a 2D system may lead to the enhancement of third-order nonlinear polarizability $\chi^{(3)}$ . In the heterostructures of conjugated MOFs stacked graphene, owing to the $\pi{\cdot}\pi$ stacking interaction, the electron clouds extension, and rapid interface charge transfer were achieved, thus increasing the conjugate length and improving the NLO properties of CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS.
## 2.4. Ultrafast Photonic Applications
In order to achieve mode-locked pulse output in fiber laser, CuNsG-VHS-SA, NiNsG-VHS-SA, and other SAs were fixed in two fiber ferrules and connected in the ring-cavity erbium-doped fiber (EDF) laser. A metallographic microscope cooperated with the dry transfer strategy was used to achieve the precise transfer of the required ultrathin materials on the optical fiber core surface. Accordingly, different types of SAs were obtained. Figure S27 (Supporting Information) reveals the configuration of the ring-cavity EDF laser.
## 2.4.1. Fundamental and Harmonic Mode-Locking Based on CuNsG-VHS
At a pump power of $76~\mathrm{mW}_{\cdot}$ a stable self-starting FML operation was appeared for CuNsG-VHS-SA EDF laser. The FML pulse sequence with pulse spacing of 20.6 ns was observed in Figure 5a. Figure 5b showed that the repetition rate was ${\approx}48.47$ MHz, which agreed with the round-trip time of the $4.25\mathrm{m}$ cavity. A high signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of 61 dB was obtained from radio frequency (RF) spectrum, indicating the excellent pulse stability. As shown in the red section of Figure $5\mathrm{g}$ , the center wavelength of $1559.96\:\mathrm{nm}$ and 3 dB bandwidth of 6.01 nm was achieved in the spectrum of FML. The FML autocorrelation trace is exhibited in Figure 5c. A femtosecond level extremely short pulse duration of 438 fs was realized via fitting the autocorrelation trace to hyperbolic secant-squared (sech2) function. By calculation, the time bandwidth product was 0.325, further suggesting the good stability of the mode-locking pulse.
Moreover, in a fiber laser with negative dispersion, the excessively high conventional soliton pulse energy can result in the laser pulse splitting, which in turn leads to an increase in the repetition frequency. According to the soliton area theorem, the pulse energy can be expressed as:[54,55 ]
$$
E_{_P}=\frac{1.1346\left|\beta_{2,a\nu g}\right|}{\Delta\tau\times\gamma_{a\nu g}}
$$
In this expression, $\beta_{2,a\nu g}$ and $\gamma_{a\nu g}$ represent the average group velocity dispersion and the average nonlinearity of the cavity, respectively, while $\Delta\tau$ denotes the full width at half maximum (FWHM) of the pulse. This indicates that a ring cavity with low dispersion or high nonlinearity helps to reduce the average soliton energy, thereby accelerating the pulse splitting process. The designed extended $\pi$ -conjugated SA materials fully meet the requirement of high nonlinearity. Therefore, when the pump power is increased, the combined effects of gain and the soliton area theorem cause the single pulse energy to reach its limit, resulting in pulse splitting and achieving the HML operation. The HML was observed for CuNsG-VHS-SA EDF laser as the increase of pump power. Figure 5d displays curves of output power and repetition rate with pump power variation. The laser changed to the HML condition as the power increased to $98\mathrm{mW}.$ The output powers and harmonic repetition rates were obviously raised after increasing the pump powers and kept a general linear relationship. The evolution process of the output spectrum is depicted in Figure 5e. By increasing the pump power, the transition process of the laser from CW to FML and then to HML was clearly observed from the image. The spectral width and effective modulation depth gradually reduced during the HML operation with the increase of pump power. Based on the oscilloscope with 50 ns time scale, the splitting processes of pulse sequences are exhibited in Figure 5f. The pulse interval decreased from 20.6 ns for FML to 0.74 ns for the highest level harmonic splitting. A highest 26-order HML with the repetition rate as high as $1.26~\mathrm{GHz}$ was achieved. The blue section in Figure $5\mathrm{g}$ showed that the center wavelength of $1560.04~\mathrm{nm}$ and $3~\mathrm{dB}$ bandwidth of $1.99\mathrm{nm}$ was obtained in the HML spectrum. As pump power increased, the damage to the SAs induced from the thermal effects led to the slight variation of the center wavelength. In Figure 5h, the
RF spectrum displayed a SNR of 51 dB, demonstrating the high laser output stability. The autocorrelation trace of 26-order HML is given in Figure 5i. The pulse duration was 1.32 ps according to sech2 function fitting and calculations.
## 2.4.2. Fundamental and Harmonic Mode-Locking Based on NiNsG-VHS
At pump power of $78\mathrm{~mW},$ a stable self-starting FML operation was appeared for NiNsG-VHS-SA EDF laser. The FML pulse sequence with pulse spacing of 20.2 ns was observed in Figure 6a. Figure 6b revealed that the repetition rate was ${\approx}49$ MHz. A high SNR of 54 dB was obtained. As shown in the purple section of Figure $6\mathrm{g}$ , the center wavelength of $1559.58~\mathrm{nm}$ and 3 dB bandwidth of $5.88\mathrm{nm}$ was achieved in the spectrum of FML. In Figure 6c, the short pulse duration of 449 fs was realized via sech2 function fitting. The HML was observed as the increase of pump power. Figure 6d displayed curves of output power and repetition rate with pump power variation. The evolution process of output spectrum is depicted in Figure 6e. The laser changed to the HML condition as the pump power reached to $91{\mathrm{~mW}}$ . The splitting processes of pulse sequences are exhibited in Figure 6f. The pulse interval decreased from 20.2 ns for FML to 1.09 ns for the highest level harmonic splitting. The highest 18-order HML with the repetition rate as high as $0.87~\mathrm{GHz}$ was achieved. The blue section in Figure $6\mathrm{g}$ showed that the center wavelength of $1560.11\mathrm{nm}$ and 3 dB bandwidth of $2.05\mathrm{nm}$ was obtained in the
18-order HML spectrum. In Figure 6h, the RF spectrum revealed a SNR of 50 dB, indicating the high laser output stability. The pulse duration was 1.18 ps according to sech2 function fitting and calculations.
## 2.4.3. Fundamental and Harmonic Mode-Locking Based on CuBG-VHS and NiBG-VHS
For CuBG-VHS-SA EDF laser, a stable self-starting FML was achieved. In Figure S28 (Supporting Information), the FML pulse sequence with a pulse interval of 20.7 ns was observed. The repetition rate was $48.37~\mathrm{MHz}$ and SNR was 54 dB. The spectrum exhibited a center wavelength of 1560.72 nm and 3 dB bandwidth of $4.74\mathrm{nm}$ . The pulse duration was 556 fs by sech2 function fitting and calculations. The 6-order HML were realized for CuBGVHS-SA with the increase of pump power. The pulse spacing decreased to 3.5 ns for the highest harmonic splitting, which corresponded to the repetition rate of 0.291 GHz. The RF spectrum displayed a SNR of 53 dB for 6-order HML, proving the high stability of laser output. Moreover, in Figure S29 (Supporting Information), NiBG-VHS achieved a stable self-starting FML with a pulse duration of 527 fs and a 2-order HML.
Additionally, the performance of CuHHTPNs-SA, NiHHTPNs-SA, CuHHTPB-SA, NiHHTPB-SA, and grapheneSA EDF lasers was also measured. The graphene-SA EDF laser only achieved the FML with a pulse duration of 695 fs and SNR of 42 dB (Figure S30, Supporting Information). HML was not obtained as the pump power increased. As shown in Figures S31–S34 (Supporting Information), the individual CuHHTPNs, NiHHTPNs, CuHHTPB, and NiHHTPB MOF-based SAs only showed the stable Q-switched pulse output. As a consequence, owing to the favorable NLO properties of the $\pi{\cdot}\pi$ stacked ultrathin MOF nanosheets/graphene heterostructures, CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS exhibited better performance than CuBG-VHS, NiBG-VHS and separate materials as the SAs for mode-locked fiber laser.
The NLO properties and laser performance of different kinds of individual 2D materials and heterostructures are summarized in Table 2 and Table S2 (Supporting Information). Compared with most materials, the CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS showed superior modulation depths, proving their outstanding NLO properties. A narrow pulse duration of 438 fs and high repetition-rate 26-order HML of $1.26~\mathrm{GHz}$ were achieved for CuNsG-VHS. In the meantime, a narrow pulse duration of 449 fs and high repetition-rate 18-order HML of 0.87 GHz were achieved for NiNsG-VHS, which were preferable than most materials. Furthermore, CuNsG-VHS-based EDF mode-locked laser exhibited a high stability with the SNR of 61 dB. The fast interface charge transfer, extended $\pi$ -conjugated electron cloud and better $\pi{\cdot}\pi$ stacking interactions jointly improved the NLO properties of CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS, making them served as excellent SAs for high-performance pulse fiber lasers.
## 3. Conclusion
The ultrathin CuHHTPNs and NiHHTPNs MOF nanosheets with a thickness of $3{\-}4\ \mathrm{nm}$ and single-crystalline characteristics were successfully prepared by surfactant-assisted solution synthesis strategy. The $\pi{\cdot}\pi$ stacked van der Waals heterostructures of CuNsG-VHS and NiNsG-VHS were synthesized via ultrasoundassisted method. The rapid charge transfer and $\pi$ -conjugated electron cloud extension were achieved at the interface between MOF and graphene. The NLO properties including the modulation depth, nonlinear absorption coefficient, and third-order nonlinear polarizability, were greatly improved after the generation of $\pi{\cdot}\pi$ stacking interactions. The $\beta$ and $\operatorname{Im}\chi^{(3)}$ values of CuNsGVHS are $-1.1\times10^{5}$ and -46.6 $(\times10^{-8})$ esu, respectively, which were the highest level among the reported 2D saturable absorber materials. Subsequently, as excellent saturable absorbers for EDF lasers, CuNsG-VHS achieved the FML with pulse duration of 438 fs, the 26-order HML with repetition frequency as high as 1.26 $\mathrm{GHz}$ , and SNR of 51 dB. NiNsG-VHS achieved the FML with a pulse duration of 449 fs, the 18-order harmonic mode-locking with a repetition frequency of $0.87~\mathrm{GHz}$ , and SNR of 50 dB. Moreover, owing to the superior interactions, the ultrathin MOFs nanosheets stacked graphene exhibited preferable NLO properties and mode-locked pulse output performance than bulk MOFs stacked graphene. This work not only provides new strategy for designing promising MOF-based SA for ultrafast photonics, but also proposes new ideas for developing high-performance NLO materials for other optoelectronic applications.
